# Unity HPC project

<!-- unity-hpc-guidance: @@UNITY_HPC_VERSION@@ -->

## Controller connection

Use `unity-hpc:connect` to attach this directory, `unity-hpc:dispatch` to submit
reviewed work, and `unity-hpc:doctor` to diagnose connection failures.
`unityHPC/controller.json` identifies the controller and registered project.
The controller calls `unityHPC/run.sh`; that script remains the ledger writer.
Humans operate the project UI. Approved workflows continue through deterministic
scripts when the browser closes.

## Project ownership

Scientific source, inputs, runners, tests, analysis, and plotting code belong to
this project. `unityHPC/project.json` declares their versioned interfaces.
Declare only implemented capabilities. Generated wrappers and review metadata
live under `.unity-workspace/`; keep source changes in project-owned entrypoints.

Keep `unityHPC/.env`, `sync.sh`, and `fetch.sh` as project-owned files. Repeated
setup preserves them. Supply explicit configuration flags to change environment
values. Preserve existing output corpora and historical ledger identities.

## Reviewing an experiment

Review expanded variants, dependencies, per-stage resources, commands, outputs,
and source/configuration references before dispatch. Repeat uses fresh output
paths. Resume and retry require compatible inputs and a separate reviewed plan.
Smoke test is a Purpose, distinct from scheduler State; exclude its records from
research aggregates. Historical jobs without purpose remain Unclassified.

Run bounded fixture and mock checks locally. Inference belongs on compute nodes.
A live cluster smoke test needs a reviewed resource and output envelope.
Connection checks and scaffold generation do not authorize a cluster workload.

## Transfer and telemetry

Use the controller for project operations and cluster access. Declared artifact
retrieval selects an execution's outputs; legacy fetch adapters retain their
existing contract. Collector deployment is opt-in. The controller owns scheduled
telemetry sessions and their lifecycle.
